r","url":"https://api.github.com/users/web-flow","user_view_type":"public"},"html_url":"https://github.com/VirtoCommerce/vc-frontend/commit/261432a07b89c9f725d19cef2195ead4dff57e6d","node_id":"C_kwDOEam6btoAKDI2MTQzMmEwN2I4OWM5ZjcyNWQxOWNlZjIxOTVlYWQ0ZGZmNTdlNmQ","parents":[{"html_url":"https://github.com/VirtoCommerce/vc-frontend/commit/b61e9398a331b650c345b33bc6736e4fa7c477da","sha":"b61e9398a331b650c345b33bc6736e4fa7c477da","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VirtoCommerce/vc-frontend/commits/b61e9398a331b650c345b33bc6736e4fa7c477da"}],"sha":"261432a07b89c9f725d19cef2195ead4dff57e6d","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VirtoCommerce/vc-frontend/commits/261432a07b89c9f725d19cef2195ead4dff57e6d"},{"author":{"avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/46611511?v=4","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/events{/privacy}","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/gists{/gist_id}","gravatar_id":"","html_url":"https://github.com/muller39","id":46611511,"login":"muller39","node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjQ2NjExNTEx","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/orgs","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/received_events","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/repos","site_admin":false,"starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39/subscriptions","type":"User","url":"https://api.github.com/users/muller39","user_view_type":"public"},"comments_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VirtoCommerce/vc-frontend/commits/b61e9398a331b650c345b33bc6736e4fa7c477da/comments","commit":{"author":{"date":"2026-09-04T15:28:37Z","email":"46611511+muller39@users.noreply.github.com","name":"Alexander Kurilin"},"comment_count":0,"committer":{"date":"2026-09-04T15:28:37Z","email":"noreply@github.com","name":"GitHub"},"message":"fix: batch rapid-fire cart line-item removals into a single mutation (#2442)\n\n## Description\n\n**Symptom.** Production Application Insights telemetry on a downstream\nfork (built on this theme) surfaced\n`DistributedLockService.ExecuteAsync` throwing `LockError: \"Service is\nbusy.\"` when a Redis RedLock on key `Cart:{userId}` can''t be acquired\nwithin its wait window. This surfaces to the shopper as a visible\n`ApolloError: Service is busy.` on the Cart page. Over 30 days: ~78\nserver-side occurrences, ~90% on `RemoveCartItems`. One burst: a single\nuser produced **21 failures in one minute, with 21 distinct Operation\nIds** — 21 different concurrent mutations, not 21 retries of the same\nclick. Failed requests do zero work: they just queue behind the lock for\nthe full wait window and throw.\n\n**Root cause.** `RemoveCartItemsCommand` already accepts `lineItemIds:\nstring[]` — a single call can remove several line items at once — but\nthe storefront never used that. The per-row trash icon emits one id per\nclick:\n\n```ts\n// client-app/ui-kit/components/molecules/line-items/vc-line-items.vue\nfunction removeSingleItem(itemId: string) {\n  emit(\"remove:items\", [itemId]);\n}\n```\n\nand `removeItems()` fired the mutation directly on every invocation,\nwith no batching. If a shopper removes several line items in the space\nof a second or two, each click fires its own network call, and they all\nrace the same per-user distributed lock concurrently instead of\ncollapsing into one call. Two other contributing layers were identified\nby the same RCA — an expensive per-mutation cart recalculation, and the\nlock''s wait-window/hold-time tuning — but both are backend-side\n(`vc-module-x-api`) and out of scope here; this PR addresses only the\nstorefront fan-out.\n\n**Fix.** Add a `RemoveCartItems` target to the existing\n`queuedMutationsLink` (already wired into the Apollo link chain and\nalready used for `UpdateShortCartItemQuantity`), instead of a\nhand-rolled debounce+abort batcher:\n\n```ts\n// client-app/core/api/graphql/config/links/queued-mutations/queued-mutations.ts\nexport const removeCartItemsConfig: IQueueTargetConfig<RemoveCartItemsMutationVariables> = {\n  debounceMs: 1000,\n  getPartitionKey: (vars) => vars.command?.cartId ?? \"\",\n  mergeQueued: (a, b) => ({\n    ...a,\n    command: {\n      ...a.command,\n      lineItemIds: [...new Set([...(a.command?.lineItemIds ?? []), ...(b.command?.lineItemIds ?? [])])],\n    },\n  }),\n};\n```\n\n`useCart.ts`''s `removeItems()` and `cart.vue`''s `handleRemoveItems()` go\nback to exactly what they were before this PR touched them — plain\n`await _removeItems(...)` with the original `optimisticResponse`. The\nbatching now lives entirely in the Apollo link chain, transparent to the\ncaller.\n\n**Why the link over a hand-rolled batcher, not just \"which one is\ncleaner.\"** An earlier version of this PR used `useMutationBatcher`\n(debounce + `AbortController`), reusing a pattern already in this file\nfor quantity-change and select/unselect. Caught in review: that\npattern''s abort only stops the *client* from waiting on an\nalready-dispatched request — it can''t un-send it, and the server keeps\nprocessing it. A click arriving after the previous request already went\nout doesn''t cancel that work; it queues a *second* request on top, so\ntwo removals can still run concurrently against the same lock — the\nexact failure mode this PR exists to fix. It also re-sends ids it\nalready sent (`[A]`, then `[A, B]`, then `[A, B, C]`, since the\nbatcher''s buffer only clears on success, not on abort), and gets\n*worse*, not better, as the server slows down: it fires at the pace of\nclicking regardless of whether the previous request has returned, so a\nslower server means more requests piled up concurrently, not fewer.\n\n`queuedMutationsLink` never cancels an in-flight request. A new click\nwhile one is out just queues; when the in-flight request settles,\neverything queued goes out together as one merged request. That gives\nthree properties the batcher can''t: at most one `RemoveCartItems` in\nflight at any click speed; no re-sent ids (each flush clears its own\nqueue); and it eases off automatically under load — a slower server\nmeans fewer, bigger merged requests, not more concurrent ones.\n\nIt also resolves every caller''s promise when the batch settles, not just\nthe last one in a burst (each queued call gets its own\n`observer.next()`/`complete()`), so `await removeItems(...)` and\n`handleRemoveItems`''s per-click analytics work exactly as they did\nbefore batching existed — no fire-and-forget workaround needed. And\nbecause nothing gets cancelled, the original `optimisticResponse`\nsurvives untouched, with Apollo''s normal automatic rollback on a failed\nmutation — no hand-written cache patch, no missing-rollback trade-off.\n\n`getPartitionKey` is one addition beyond reusing the existing pattern\nas-is: unlike `UpdateShortCartItemQuantity` (a global singleton always\nscoped to the user''s own cart), `removeItems()` lives in\n`_useFullCart(cartId?)`, which also serves the SharedCart route (viewing\na specific shared cart by id). Without partitioning, the link would key\nits queue purely by operation name, so two calls for two different carts\nlanding in the same debounce window could merge into a single request\ncarrying only one of the two `cartId`s — silently applying half the\nremoval to the wrong cart. Partitioning by `cartId` keeps the user''s own\ncart on the default queue (empty string, same as omitting the key) while\ngiving any other cart its own independent queue.\n\n## Tests\n\nAdded three tests directly against the shipped `removeCartItemsConfig`\n(exported for this purpose) in `queued-mutations.test.ts`, each driven\nthrough a fresh, isolated controller rather than the shared production\nsingleton:\n- merges and dedupes `lineItemIds` from multiple calls on the same cart\ninto one request (including a duplicate id sent twice),\n- never merges removals from two different `cartId`s into one request,\n- routes the user''s own cart (no `cartId`) to the same default queue\nregardless of call order.\n\n`queuedMutationsLink`''s generic debounce/merge/partition/flush mechanics\nwere already covered by the existing 38 tests in this file; these three\nexist because that generic coverage says nothing about whether *this*\ntarget''s own merge function and partition key actually do the right\nthing.\n\nNo new test for `useCart.ts`/`cart.vue` — both are back to their pre-PR\ncode, already exercised implicitly wherever cart removal already worked\nbefore this change.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `eslint` on changed files — clean.\n- `vue-tsc --build --force` — clean.\n- `vitest run` — all passing (81 cart-composable tests + 41 in\n`queued-mutations.test.ts`, including the 3 new ones).\n- Manually verified against a running dev instance: added multiple line\nitems, removed them individually down to an empty cart, confirmed\ninstant UI removal via the restored `optimisticResponse`, correct\nrecalculated totals, and correct final cart state after a full page\nreload. Also confirmed per-click analytics now fire independently for\neach removal rather than collapsing to the last click in a burst\n(visible via distinct debug log entries), which the earlier\nbatcher-based version could silently drop.\n- Did **not** manage to empirically reproduce two clicks landing inside\nthe same 1s debounce window through browser automation (tool round-trip\nlatency between simulated clicks exceeded 1s, so each fired its own\nindependent, correctly-handled request) — the merge/partition behavior\nabove is verified by the new unit tests instead, not by a live repro of\nthat specific timing window.\n\n## Notes for reviewers\n\n- **Not load-tested against a live distributed lock.** This removes the\nstorefront''s own contribution to the concurrency the original RCA\nmeasured (and does so more strongly than a debounce-based approach — see\nabove); it does not by itself guarantee the production `LockError`\ndisappears, since two other contributing layers (backend\ncart-recalculation cost, lock wait-window/hold-time) are explicitly out\nof scope and unaffected by this PR. That end-to-end validation is being\ntracked separately by the team that reported the original production\nincident.\n- **Only serializes a mutation against itself.** The distributed lock is\nper user, not per operation, so `RemoveCartItems` can still race an\n`AddItem` or a quantity change for the same cart — this PR (and the\nqueued-link approach in general) only guarantees at most one\n`RemoveCartItems` in flight, not at most one cart mutation of any kind.\n- **No `overflowed`-equivalent UX guard.** The previous version disabled\nfurther removal input after 10 pending ids; `queuedMutationsLink` has no\nsuch cap. The closest available signal is\n`queuedTotal`/`hasQueuedMutations` from `useQueuedMutations()`, already\nused for the cross-page \"you have pending changes\" leave-guard. Not\nwired into a per-field disable here, matching the fact that the\nalready-shipped `UpdateShortCartItemQuantity` usage doesn''t have one\neither.\n\n## References\n### Jira-link:\n<!-- Put link to your task in Jira here -->\n### Artifact URL:\n\nhttps://vc3prerelease.blob.core.windows.net/packages/vc-theme-b2b-vue-2.57.0-pr-2442-29ed-29ed39a5.zip\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Elena Mutykova <56412054+Lenajava1@users.noreply.github.com>","tree":{"sha":"0a06602b6986b83ea2440f56b0f82d43242d24f5","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VirtoCommerce/vc-frontend/git/trees/0a06602b6986b83ea2440f56b0f82d43242d24f5"},"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/VirtoCommerce/vc-frontend/git/commits/b61e9398a331b650c345b33bc6736e4fa7c477da","verification":{"payload":"tree 0a06602b6986b83ea2440f56b0f82d43242d24f5\nparent c4046f560a35fe0b9f260f2ef353129792b45c98\nauthor Alexander Kurilin <46611511+muller39@users.noreply.github.com> 1788535717 +0200\ncommitter GitHub <noreply@github.com> 1788535717 +0200\n\nfix: batch rapid-fire cart line-item removals into a single mutation (#2442)\n\n## Description\n\n**Symptom.** Production Application Insights telemetry on a downstream\nfork (built on this theme) surfaced\n`DistributedLockService.ExecuteAsync` throwing `LockError: \"Service is\nbusy.\"` when a Redis RedLock on key `Cart:{userId}` can''t be acquired\nwithin its wait window. This surfaces to the shopper as a visible\n`ApolloError: Service is busy.` on the Cart page. Over 30 days: ~78\nserver-side occurrences, ~90% on `RemoveCartItems`. One burst: a single\nuser produced **21 failures in one minute, with 21 distinct Operation\nIds** — 21 different concurrent mutations, not 21 retries of the same\nclick. Failed requests do zero work: they just queue behind the lock for\nthe full wait window and throw.\n\n**Root cause.** `RemoveCartItemsCommand` already accepts `lineItemIds:\nstring[]` — a single call can remove several line items at once — but\nthe storefront never used that. The per-row trash icon emits one id per\nclick:\n\n```ts\n// client-app/ui-kit/components/molecules/line-items/vc-line-items.vue\nfunction removeSingleItem(itemId: string) {\n  emit(\"remove:items\", [itemId]);\n}\n```\n\nand `removeItems()` fired the mutation directly on every invocation,\nwith no batching. If a shopper removes several line items in the space\nof a second or two, each click fires its own network call, and they all\nrace the same per-user distributed lock concurrently instead of\ncollapsing into one call. Two other contributing layers were identified\nby the same RCA — an expensive per-mutation cart recalculation, and the\nlock''s wait-window/hold-time tuning — but both are backend-side\n(`vc-module-x-api`) and out of scope here; this PR addresses only the\nstorefront fan-out.\n\n**Fix.** Add a `RemoveCartItems` target to the existing\n`queuedMutationsLink` (already wired into the Apollo link chain and\nalready used for `UpdateShortCartItemQuantity`), instead of a\nhand-rolled debounce+abort batcher:\n\n```ts\n// client-app/core/api/graphql/config/links/queued-mutations/queued-mutations.ts\nexport const removeCartItemsConfig: IQueueTargetConfig<RemoveCartItemsMutationVariables> = {\n  debounceMs: 1000,\n  getPartitionKey: (vars) => vars.command?.cartId ?? \"\",\n  mergeQueued: (a, b) => ({\n    ...a,\n    command: {\n      ...a.command,\n      lineItemIds: [...new Set([...(a.command?.lineItemIds ?? []), ...(b.command?.lineItemIds ?? [])])],\n    },\n  }),\n};\n```\n\n`useCart.ts`''s `removeItems()` and `cart.vue`''s `handleRemoveItems()` go\nback to exactly what they were before this PR touched them — plain\n`await _removeItems(...)` with the original `optimisticResponse`. The\nbatching now lives entirely in the Apollo link chain, transparent to the\ncaller.\n\n**Why the link over a hand-rolled batcher, not just \"which one is\ncleaner.\"** An earlier version of this PR used `useMutationBatcher`\n(debounce + `AbortController`), reusing a pattern already in this file\nfor quantity-change and select/unselect. Caught in review: that\npattern''s abort only stops the *client* from waiting on an\nalready-dispatched request — it can''t un-send it, and the server keeps\nprocessing it. A click arriving after the previous request already went\nout doesn''t cancel that work; it queues a *second* request on top, so\ntwo removals can still run concurrently against the same lock — the\nexact failure mode this PR exists to fix. It also re-sends ids it\nalready sent (`[A]`, then `[A, B]`, then `[A, B, C]`, since the\nbatcher''s buffer only clears on success, not on abort), and gets\n*worse*, not better, as the server slows down: it fires at the pace of\nclicking regardless of whether the previous request has returned, so a\nslower server means more requests piled up concurrently, not fewer.\n\n`queuedMutationsLink` never cancels an in-flight request. A new click\nwhile one is out just queues; when the in-flight request settles,\neverything queued goes out together as one merged request. That gives\nthree properties the batcher can''t: at most one `RemoveCartItems` in\nflight at any click speed; no re-sent ids (each flush clears its own\nqueue); and it eases off automatically under load — a slower server\nmeans fewer, bigger merged requests, not more concurrent ones.\n\nIt also resolves every caller''s promise when the batch settles, not just\nthe last one in a burst (each queued call gets its own\n`observer.next()`/`complete()`), so `await removeItems(...)` and\n`handleRemoveItems`''s per-click analytics work exactly as they did\nbefore batching existed — no fire-and-forget workaround needed. And\nbecause nothing gets cancelled, the original `optimisticResponse`\nsurvives untouched, with Apollo''s normal automatic rollback on a failed\nmutation — no hand-written cache patch, no missing-rollback trade-off.\n\n`getPartitionKey` is one addition beyond reusing the existing pattern\nas-is: unlike `UpdateShortCartItemQuantity` (a global singleton always\nscoped to the user''s own cart), `removeItems()` lives in\n`_useFullCart(cartId?)`, which also serves the SharedCart route (viewing\na specific shared cart by id). Without partitioning, the link would key\nits queue purely by operation name, so two calls for two different carts\nlanding in the same debounce window could merge into a single request\ncarrying only one of the two `cartId`s — silently applying half the\nremoval to the wrong cart. Partitioning by `cartId` keeps the user''s own\ncart on the default queue (empty string, same as omitting the key) while\ngiving any other cart its own independent queue.\n\n## Tests\n\nAdded three tests directly against the shipped `removeCartItemsConfig`\n(exported for this purpose) in `queued-mutations.test.ts`, each driven\nthrough a fresh, isolated controller rather than the shared production\nsingleton:\n- merges and dedupes `lineItemIds` from multiple calls on the same cart\ninto one request (including a duplicate id sent twice),\n- never merges removals from two different `cartId`s into one request,\n- routes the user''s own cart (no `cartId`) to the same default queue\nregardless of call order.\n\n`queuedMutationsLink`''s generic debounce/merge/partition/flush mechanics\nwere already covered by the existing 38 tests in this file; these three\nexist because that generic coverage says nothing about whether *this*\ntarget''s own merge function and partition key actually do the right\nthing.\n\nNo new test for `useCart.ts`/`cart.vue` — both are back to their pre-PR\ncode, already exercised implicitly wherever cart removal already worked\nbefore this change.\n\n## Verification\n\n- `eslint` on changed files — clean.\n- `vue-tsc --build --force` — clean.\n- `vitest run` — all passing (81 cart-composable tests + 41 in\n`queued-mutations.test.ts`, including the 3 new ones).\n- Manually verified against a running dev instance: added multiple line\nitems, removed them individually down to an empty cart, confirmed\ninstant UI removal via the restored `optimisticResponse`, correct\nrecalculated totals, and correct final cart state after a full page\nreload. Also confirmed per-click analytics now fire independently for\neach removal rather than collapsing to the last click in a burst\n(visible via distinct debug log entries), which the earlier\nbatcher-based version could silently drop.\n- Did **not** manage to empirically reproduce two clicks landing inside\nthe same 1s debounce window through browser automation (tool round-trip\nlatency between simulated clicks exceeded 1s, so each fired its own\nindependent, correctly-handled request) — the merge/partition behavior\nabove is verified by the new unit tests instead, not by a live repro of\nthat specific timing window.\n\n## Notes for reviewers\n\n- **Not load-tested against a live distributed lock.** This removes the\nstorefront''s own contribution to the concurrency the original RCA\nmeasured (and does so more strongly than a debounce-based approach — see\nabove); it does not by itself guarantee the production `LockError`\ndisappears, since two other contributing layers (backend\ncart-recalculation cost, lock wait-window/hold-time) are explicitly out\nof scope and unaffected by this PR. That end-to-end validation is being\ntracked separately by the team that reported the original production\nincident.\n- **Only serializes a mutation against itself.** The distributed lock is\nper user, not per operation, so `RemoveCartItems` can still race an\n`AddItem` or a quantity change for the same cart — this PR (and the\nqueued-link approach in general) only guarantees at most one\n`RemoveCartItems` in flight, not at most one cart mutation of any kind.\n- **No `overflowed`-equivalent UX guard.** The previous version disabled\nfurther removal input after 10 pending ids; `queuedMutationsLink` has no\nsuch cap. The closest available signal is\n`queuedTotal`/`hasQueuedMutations` from `useQueuedMutations()`, already\nused for the cross-page \"you have pending changes\" leave-guard. Not\nwired into a per-field disable here, matching the fact that the\nalready-shipped `UpdateShortCartItemQuantity` usage doesn''t have one\neither.\n\n## References\n### Jira-link:\n<!-- Put link to your task in Jira here -->\n### Artifact URL:\n\nhttps://vc3prerelease.blob.core.windows.net/packages/vc-theme-b2b-vue-2.57.0-pr-2442-29ed-29ed39a5.zip\n\n---------\n\nCo-authored-by: Elena Mutykova <56412054+Lenajava1@users.noreply.github.com>","reason":"valid","signature":"-----BEGIN PGP SIGNATURE-----\n\nwsFcBAABCAAQBQJqmuOlCRC1aQ7uu5UhlAAAOX4QABqTrFxRkhR6w4VwibOo0B0O\nf0vZu5AtSkcxHmVe6jp+bvRuhJIyH5RqW5qwPunMpp+75Y9kf+2Euhwjv+H3wbE3\nEncMdiViZaqCDzyHGt9GAqCKmZhyWHFpuGtoCQZUD6A2XlzkQtw9sUjxx1hJSb4c\n4ndREb7jqnhB+DNkh4CHB7gvZwwmy9C4c4wjBBXmIdU3c4aX16K0nuPeRF3gq4IN\nyzpaxJAQpY7rnnNbW01SBfpfkBcBr3MxODKJGsDwn2L/Ds8V7mJaY/MG/AK4hSzU\n/TCdiov8ajxOHU7jsiNgsVTesd+PD4TxD4bY/pc71OmG/uuLwr